concept_mcqs.stem:
Consider a transaction with savepoints:
```sql
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO invoices(id, amount) VALUES (1, 100);
SAVEPOINT s1;
INSERT INTO invoices(id, amount) VALUES (1, 200); -- violates PK
ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT s1;
INSERT INTO invoices(id, amount) VALUES (2, 200);
COMMIT;
```
Assuming no other constraints, what is the final state?

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Use a single transaction per EOD batch with explicit lock ordering (e.g., lock affected `orders` rows, then `inventory`, then `ledger` entries). Keep isolation at REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE only if needed; otherwise READ COMMITTED suffices if correctness is tied to FK integrity and application-level invariants. Make FKs `DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED` on write-heavy paths and issue `SET CONSTRAINTS ALL DEFERRED;` at the start so FK checks occur at commit, reducing mid-transaction blocking. Use savepoints around batches to recover from per-row issues without aborting the whole run. Example:
```sql
BEGIN;
SET CONSTRAINTS ALL DEFERRED;
-- process in deterministic order by order_id to avoid deadlocks
-- optionally: SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED to fetch work items
-- post inventory and ledger in fixed order
SAVEPOINT batch1;
-- ... DML ...
-- on error: ROLLBACK TO batch1;
COMMIT;
```
Explain that deferring FKs reduces conflicting reads, consistent lock ordering reduces deadlocks, and savepoints localize failures. To reduce serialization conflicts with reporting, keep transactions short, avoid long-running snapshots, and consider breaking the job into smaller atomic chunks processed sequentially.
